# {{chiefly|British|Australia|and|New Zealand}} {{context|of food}} to be [[eaten]] off the [[premises]]

====Usage notes====
Frequently used in the question “eat-in or '''takeaway'''?” (North American: “for here or to go?”) at restaurants that offer food for both on and off premise eating.

====Synonyms====
* [[carryout]] {{qualifier|Scotland, US}}
* [[takeout]] {{qualifier|chiefly North America}}
* [[to go]] {{qualifier|North America}}

====Antonyms====
* [[eat in]] {{qualifier|British}}
* [[for here]] {{qualifier|North America}}
* [[have here]] {{qualifier|New Zealand}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{chiefly|British|Australia|and|New Zealand}} a [[restaurant]] that sells takeaway food
#: ''If you're hungry, there's a '''takeaway''' just around the corner.''
# {{chiefly|British|Australia|and|New Zealand}} a [[meal]] that was bought from a takeaway
#: ''I fancy an Indian '''takeaway''' tonight.''
# {{US}} a [[concession]] made by a [[labor]] [[union]] in the course of [[negotiation]]s
# {{US}} an [[idea]] to be [[take away|taken away]] by the [[listener]] after a [[talk]] or [[presentation]]

